            <ArticleTitle>Initial results and long-term clinical and angiographic implications of coronary stenting in elderly patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1483-7, A7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Results of 378 consecutive elderly patients (&gt; or = 65 years) undergoing coronary stenting were compared with those of 601 younger patients. Although the restenosis rate was similar in the 2 groups, age &gt; or = 65 years was an independent predictor of in-hospital mortality (relative risk 5.4, 95% confidence interval 1.2 to 20.1) and follow-up mortality (relative risk 2.8, 95% confidence interval 1.3 to 6.1).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
